From eaf6a85346309ee3c8f0ec18a61e0643e0280096 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (sandbox)" <claude@sandbox.local>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 13:29:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Cohort assembly: obligation_sweep.py -- the primary-side
 sweep for C2 dimensions 4+5

Runs on the primary, not the twin (see module docstring for why): the
twin holds obligation resolution state, the primary already holds
decision input_fields via material_from_ledger_row, and shipping raw
input fields to the twin in the clear just to compute dimension 5 there
would be a much bigger, more sensitive data-sharing change than domain
ever was. The twin still never computes a cassette's judgment anywhere
in this codebase; this keeps that pattern.

Pure logic (bucket_resolved_obligations, assemble_cohort, review_cohort)
has no I/O and is fully unit tested. Buckets by (domain, obligation_kind)
-- not obligation_kind alone, per the earlier domain fix. Each obligation
is evaluated independently for dimension 4 vs dimension 5 eligibility
(different data requirements), and anything that can't enter a cohort
test is reported in the review's skipped list with why, never silently
dropped. A cohort below MIN_COHORT_SIZE_FOR_STATISTICAL_TEST is not
pre-filtered out -- both check functions report their own INDETERMINATE
finding, which is itself the honest state for the review to carry.

I/O wrappers (fetch_resolved_obligations, fetch_group_distributions,
fetch_decision_materials, sweep) are thin and independently swappable in
tests. End-to-end test proves the full wiring: real twin instance, real
sealed channel, real ledger, two domains sharing an obligation_kind,
correctly kept in separate cohorts.

17 new tests, all against real infrastructure (no mocked governance
code). Full suite 574 passed / 6 skipped. ruff and bandit clean.
